The shift moves the whole dataset without changing its **spread**, so $\sigma_x$ is **unchanged**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (9.6,0);
\fill (1,0) circle (0.11);
\fill (2,0) circle (0.11);
\fill (3,0) circle (0.11);
\fill (4,0) circle (0.11);
\fill (5,0) circle (0.11);
\draw[->] (3,-0.9) -- (3,-0.12);
\node[below] at (3,-0.95) {$\bar{x}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (0.1,0.55) {original data};
\draw[->] (0,-2.6) -- (9.6,-2.6);
\fill (4,-2.6) circle (0.11);
\fill (5,-2.6) circle (0.11);
\fill (6,-2.6) circle (0.11);
\fill (7,-2.6) circle (0.11);
\fill (8,-2.6) circle (0.11);
\draw[->] (6,-3.5) -- (6,-2.72);
\node[below] at (6,-3.55) {$\bar{x}+k$};
\node[anchor=west] at (0.1,-2.05) {each value $+k$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Adding a value at the mean leaves the mean unchanged. Standard deviation decreases if there is spread; otherwise it stays $0$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (9.6,0);
\fill (1,0) circle (0.11);
\fill (2,0) circle (0.11);
\fill (3,0) circle (0.11);
\fill (4,0) circle (0.11);
\fill (5,0) circle (0.11);
\filldraw[fill=white,thick] (3,0.42) circle (0.11);
\node[anchor=south] at (3,0.62) {new value};
\draw[->] (3,-0.9) -- (3,-0.12);
\node[below] at (3,-0.95) {$\bar{x}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Adding a **single new value much further from the mean than the current standard deviation** increases $\sigma_x$.

